> Text Modification and Filtering

Linux includes `grep`, `sed`, `awk`, and `cut` for filtering and modifying text.

all these can work on files directly or in pipes (see topic 205):
`somecommand | grep "pattern"`
`somecommand | grep "pattern" | grep -v "blah"` (has pattern, no blah)

combining: `somecommand | grep "pattern" | sed 's/pat/lec/' | awk '{print $1,$2,$3}'`

### Searching with `grep`

`grep "pattern" <filename>`: prints lines containing "pattern".
`grep -i "pattern" <filename>`: case-insensitive search.
`grep -v "pattern" <filename>`: excludes lines matching "pattern".
`grep -r "pattern" <directory>`: recursive search in directory.
`grep -n "pattern" <filename>`: shows line numbers.

### Modifying with `sed`

`sed 's/old/new/' <filename>`: replaces first occurrence per line.
`sed 's/old/new/g' <filename>`: replaces all occurrences per line.
`sed -i 's/old/new/g' <filename>`: edit file in-place (permanently saves).

use different delimiters if needed: `sed 's|/path/to|/new/path|g'`

### Extracting Columns with `cut`

`cut -d',' -f1 <filename>`: extracts first field from comma-delimited file.
`cut -d',' -f1,3,5 <filename>`: extracts multiple fields.
`cut -c1-5 <filename>`: extracts characters 1-5 from each line.

### Processing Text with `awk`

`awk '{print $1}' <filename>`: prints first field per line (default: whitespace delimiter).
`awk -F',' '{print $2}' <filename>`: comma delimiter, print second field.
`awk '/pattern/ {print $0}' <filename>`: prints lines matching pattern.
`awk '{print NF}' <filename>`: prints number of fields per line.
